course: dictionary
lesson: 8527
title: The savanna: wide grassland and few trees
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A savanna is a large, flat area of grassland with only a few trees scattered about. You can work this out from the words around it: «On the real savanna zebras, giraffes and wildebeest graze side by side.» Graze means eating grass, and the animals are all in one wide open place.
explain: Context can also show you what a word is not. «Lions live mainly on African grassland and savanna, not in jungles.» A jungle is a thick forest full of trees, so the savanna must be open land with far fewer trees.
explain: You may also see savanna spelled savannah. Both spellings are correct and mean the same thing. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: does «lions live on a large, flat grassland» still make sense? If yes, you have it.
fact: A savanna is a large, flat grassland with only a few scattered trees.
fact: Savannas are found in hot places that have a wet season and a dry season.
fact: The African savanna is home to lions, zebras, giraffes, elephants and wildebeest.
fact: Savannas are also found in Australia, South America and India.
fact: In a food chain, grass feeds a zebra, and a zebra feeds a lion: grass → zebra → lion.
exercise: What is a savanna? | A cold mountain covered in snow | A deep lake full of fish | *A large, flat grassland with only a few scattered trees | A thick jungle full of tall trees
exercise: In the food chain grass → zebra → lion, which animal eats the grass? | Lion | *Zebra | Grass
exercise: Which animal lives on the African savanna? | *Giraffe | Penguin | Dolphin | Polar bear
test: Besides Africa, where else are savannas found? | The middle of the ocean | The top of Mount Everest | *Australia | Antarctica
test: Savannas have a wet season and which other season? | A snowy season | A season with no rain or sun ever | A windy season with no seasons at all | *A dry season
exam: Why do animals migrate on the savanna? | To find the sea | *To follow the rains | To hide in tall trees | To sleep all year round
